  "review_text": "<b>Best Panel of the Year</b>\n\n[ASSET: center | DocOckFrontLit | width=50%]\n[LINK:1963:ASM_3|ASM #3]&nbsp;     front lit Doctor Octopus\n\n\n<b>Biggest Blunder</b>\n\n[ASSET: center | DocOckSuperMan | width=50%]\n[LINK:1963:ASM_3|ASM #3]&nbsp;     Doc Ock calls Spider-Man \"Super-Man\"\n\n<b>Best Back-Up Story</b>\nThere were actually some pretty good back-up tales to be found buried amidst this year's batch of books.\n\n[HANG0] [LINK:1963:TOS_44_iron0|TOS #44] -- \"One of Us Must Die\"\n[HANG0] TOS #47 -- \"Shock!\"\n[HANG0] TTA #51 -- \"No Place to Turn!\"\n\nBut none of these held a candle to [LINK:1963:TTA_47_ant0|TTA #47]'s \"Target...Earth!\"&nbsp; However, since that one was primarily best for its laugh-out-loud moment, see Best Belly-Laugh Moment award elsewhere.\n\nFrom the candidates above however, I give the nod to:&nbsp; xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\n\n<b>Best Belly-Laugh Moment</b>\n\nThis award actually goes to the back-up tale in [LINK:1963:TTA_47_ant0|TTA #47] called \"Target...Earth!\" (by Stan Lee and Steve Ditko).\n\nVery much in line with one of the 1961's Trope Trifecta, this one detailed Ba-Korr and the warriors of the planet Klang, who are looking for a new world to conquer, and lo and behold they find \"a weak planet named Earth! (<i>Oh no!</i>)&nbsp; \"in a far-off galaxy\" no less!&nbsp; Ba-Korr sends Sagar, his mightiest warrior to scout Earth's defenses... and get <i>this</i> -- if he doesn't report back within 48 hours, the invasion will be called off!\n\nAnd so, with only 48 hours to do his scouting job, Sagar is somehow able to travel to the \"far-off galaxy\" and find that Earth will prove no match for their forces, but he never reports back, so the invasion is called off.&nbsp; Why, you ask?&nbsp; Because of where he parked his ship.&nbsp; When he goes back to his ship to signal to Ba-Korr that the invasion should proceed as planned, he finds his ship gone -- he's trapped!\n\nThe story's final panel that reveals why the ship disappeared became the year's best laugh-out-loud moment.\n[ASSET: center | SlowSinkingQuicksand | width=67%]\n\nHe parked his space ship in a bog of \"slow-sinking quicksand.\"&nbsp; And to add to the humor, he concludes that, \"There is but one thing to do... I must <i>follow</i> my ship to the bottom of this bog -- to remain there for all eternity -- to pay for my fatal error!\"&nbsp; <i>OMG!&nbsp; </i>What a silly story, but one with such an ironic ending that I burst out laughing!&nbsp; <i>Hahahaha!</i>\n\nOnce again, these aliens from \"far-off\" galaxies somehow are able to detect Earth and want to conquer it, even though there must be countless planets in their <i>own</i> galaxies that would be much more convenient!&nbsp; But, perhaps not!&nbsp; I mean, if you're able to travel to Earth <i>and</i> back within a mere 48 hours, despite the distance, perhaps <i>any</i> planet is convenient!\n\n[ASSET: right | CreepyTinkerer | width=12%] However, I also want to give out an Honorable Mention for the face of the Tinkerer in the second story that made up [LINK:1963:ASM_2_asm1|ASM #2].&nbsp; Steve Ditko strikes just the right look for a character who turns out to be every bit as creepy as this panel shows him to be.\n[BR]\n[BR] (Not only that, but -- you guessed it -- he's also an alien who's come to subjugate Earth!)"
}